WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.936 --> 00:00:06.292
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.290
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Good morning, everyone.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:12.240
Welcome to our Cloud OnAir
webinar from Google Cloud.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.680
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.980
My name is Priyanka
Vergadia, and today I

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.830
will be taking Lisa with us and
talking about Machine Learning

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.000
101.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.080
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.100
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer your--

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.597
answer your questions
during and after.

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:33.930
So with that, let's get started.

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.560
Lisa, over to you.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.260
LISA QUERA: Hi, I'm
Lisa Quera, and I'm

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.200
a Machine Learning Specialist in
the customer engineering team.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.480
And hopefully, what we'll
accomplish together today

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.370
is a sense of what
machine learning is,

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.610
what are some of the decision
parameters around it,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.620
and how you can make it
useful in your business

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.820
processes to solve problems
and capture opportunities.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.990
The first thing we need to
do is establish some terms.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.540
So artificial intelligence
is the science

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.670
to make things smart.

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:05.910
So I often actually prefer the
term "augmented intelligence,"

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.210
because you're adding a
layer of smartness to things.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:13.140
Machine learning is a
way of teaching machines

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.230
how to solve problems
on their own.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:18.324
You're enabling them
to learn and be smart.

00:01:18.324 --> 00:01:19.740
And then deep
learning is a subset

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:21.120
of that, which is
some techniques

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.260
to do machine learning.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.580
Now this is the
really important part

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.710
is that this is a new paradigm.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:33.880
We've spent decades, in
a very controlled way,

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.800
teaching computers in a very
explicit rules with programming

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.160
language.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:45.490
The new paradigm is to help the
machine learn from examples,

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:48.160
so we're not controlling
it from the top down.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.560
We're letting patterns and rules
emerge from the data itself.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.340
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So Lisa, this
sounds really, really simple

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:59.530
when I look at it
in this example.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:01.820
Is it really that simple?

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.600
LISA QUERA: No, it's not.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.610
There are several deep
learning approaches

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:08.740
that can get pretty technical.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.750
Today, because we have
such a diverse audience,

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:12.250
we're going to do
our best to make sure

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.541
that what we're talking about
is accessible to everyone

00:02:14.541 --> 00:02:18.110
and is applicable
across industries.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.425
So let's first look at
supervised learning,

00:02:20.425 --> 00:02:22.550
which is probably the most
common thing that people

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:23.260
understand.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:23.830
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Are we going to look

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:25.464
at an example, an exciting one?

00:02:25.464 --> 00:02:27.880
LISA QUERA: I am going to use
one of my favorite examples,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.110
because--

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.474
I'm going to use training a dog.

00:02:30.474 --> 00:02:31.640
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Oh, yeah.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:32.890
LISA QUERA: Because
it's actually not

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.470
so different for a dog to
learn from a machine to learn,

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.860
and you'll see why
pretty quickly.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.760
With supervised learning,
what you're doing

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.610
is you're providing input
and output examples,

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:44.770
so you're not only
providing the input data,

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:47.230
but you're also providing
the targets that

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:50.050
is associated with that.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:53.680
Now if you think about applying
that to training a dog,

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.670
you help the dog understand the
connection between a command

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:58.840
and an action.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.590
And when they-- you make it
very explicit, so you tell them,

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.540
"sit," and then when they
sit, they get a treat.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.860
So they make that very explicit
connection between the command

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.660
and the target action.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:15.940
And you do that over
many, many, many examples.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:17.470
And you guide them
along the way.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.840
As they get close to it,
you give them feedback,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.400
and you help them adjust
to creating that outcome.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.260
Now unsupervised-- if you think
about how supervised learning,

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.260
you can probably guess
how unsupervised learning

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:32.500
works is you have the data, but
you don't provide those labels.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:34.740
You don't provide that target.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.090
Now, again, think about
how a dog might learn.

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.280
A dog can learn
from observing you.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.650
They watch you day
in and day out,

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.100
and they observe from those
examples of your behavior

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.920
and come to their own ability
to predict what comes next.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.400
So if I pick up my
keys and the leash,

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.620
he gets excited, because
he's able to predict

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.120
that he gets to come.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.640
But if I pick up the keys,
and I don't pick up the leash,

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.056
you can see from
the body language

00:03:59.056 --> 00:04:00.965
that he has predicted
he has to stay home.

00:04:00.965 --> 00:04:02.173
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah, sad.

00:04:02.173 --> 00:04:04.120
LISA QUERA: Now we
didn't teach him that.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.470
That was something he learned.

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:08.470
It was unsupervised
learning from observations

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:09.932
of data points.

00:04:09.932 --> 00:04:11.890
Now reinforcement learning
is a little bit more

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.950
like supervised learning,
except that the reward is

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.430
delayed over time.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.970
So in reinforcement
learning, you have a state,

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:22.124
you have actions, and then
you have a delayed reward.

00:04:22.124 --> 00:04:24.040
So we're going to stick
with training our dog,

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:25.498
and this time,
we're going to train

00:04:25.498 --> 00:04:27.280
our dog on a full
obstacle course instead

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.120
of a single command.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.180
So as the dog is running through
and having a point of being,

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.570
a state at any point in time
along the obstacle course,

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.980
we provide verbal feedback for
either doing the next action,

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.980
being a legal action
or an illegal action

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:46.390
along the course.

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:48.400
And we guide them with
that verbal feedback

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.960
until they get to the win at the
end, when they get that reward.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:55.960
So reinforcement learning is
feedback guided along the way

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.330
until you get to the
successful outcome,

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.570
but that successful
outcome is delayed a bit.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:01.520
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.240
Makes sense.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:07.150
LISA QUERA: Now we use machine
learning in everything we do.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:08.980
Sundar calls us an
AI-first company,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.480
and what he means
by that is that we

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.570
try to use machine learning
in every possible way

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.730
to enhance our
customer experiences

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.702
and make our products better.

00:05:17.702 --> 00:05:19.660
And so if we think about
some of the techniques

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:22.060
that we just talked about
and how they might show up

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.130
in some of our products--

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.590
let's take Google Photos.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.820
Would that be supervised or
unsupervised, do you think?

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:32.230
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Supervised,
because it's got labels.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:33.130
LISA QUERA: Exactly.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:35.350
When you upload your
image, and we automatically

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:38.830
suggest labels for you,
that's because we trained it

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.980
on a data set of hundreds
of millions of images

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.620
with the associated labels.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.290
And so that's able
to be able to--

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:48.190
I think there's what?

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:50.480
20,000 labels that we
associated with that,

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.070
so it's able to make your
life easier by having

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:56.380
the machine take on that load.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.470
Now Gmail, Smart Reply--
what do you think?

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.194
Supervised or unsupervised?

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:02.860
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
That is unsupervised,

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:06.130
because there must be
a large amount of data

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.760
that it's looking at,
and then not just data

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.780
from my emails and my
replies, but also from all

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:18.220
the other emails and
replies, and then identifying

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:21.490
what are the different-- what
are the patterns on the reply?

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:23.537
So I would say unsupervised.

00:06:23.537 --> 00:06:24.370
LISA QUERA: Exactly.

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:27.730
It's identifying patterns
from massive amounts of data,

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:31.480
because trying to come up
with the input-output examples

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.390
for every possible
permutation of language

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:35.900
would be really hard.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:39.220
So that's a great example of
using unsupervised learning.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.440
And then everyone's pretty
aware of the big AlphaGo

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.550
win against Lee Se-Dol with
the global champion in Go,

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.790
and the AlphaGo won
four out of five games.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:51.550
And it did that by playing
hundreds of millions of games

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.851
and getting feedback
along that process.

00:06:54.851 --> 00:06:56.725
But we don't just use
it in our own products.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.340
But we use these
models together,

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:06.120
so one input is often the
output to another model,

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.240
because a model solves
one problem really well.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.500
But most business processes have
multiple tasks along the way.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.300
So you have to have
models work together,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.780
and here's a great example
of the Vision API being

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.630
used to capture the
text from an image,

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:25.410
and then passing that to another
model with the Translate API

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:28.782
to convert that text
to another language.

00:07:28.782 --> 00:07:30.490
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I
just had a question.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:31.990
Can you combine--
like in this case,

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:35.050
we're combining two
models together.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.940
How many of these models can
you actually combine together?

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.186
Is there a limit on this?

00:07:39.186 --> 00:07:41.560
LISA QUERA: The limit wouldn't
be from a machine learning

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.160
perspective.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:43.780
The limit would
actually probably

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.670
be more from your
infrastructure perspective,

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:49.420
so the networking or the
compute power behind it

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:53.090
to pass those inputs and
outputs along the process,

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:55.270
so how long would that chain be?

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.125
So if you needed fast
latency, then you'd

00:07:57.125 --> 00:07:59.500
probably have to limit the
number of models that you use.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.110
But that's not a machine
learning question.

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:04.240
That's more of an
infrastructure question.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.850
So you just have to think
through your business process

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:10.540
and think, which tasks can the
machine enhance along the way?

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.870
But we're also using
machine learning to help

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.900
other people do amazing things.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.870
It's not just about
products and services.

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.920
It's also about trying to use
machine learning to make things

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.050
better for people.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:24.900
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, so this one's

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:27.610
actually really close to my
heart, because I'm from India,

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:31.740
and I have seen a lot
of rural places in India

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:36.789
that do not have access to
doctors or, say, good doctors.

00:08:36.789 --> 00:08:39.390
But what Google
Brain has done is

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:42.679
they've built this software
based on machine learning,

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:45.630
and there's this model
that identifies--

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:48.600
when you feed retina
scans to this model,

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.540
it identifies a disease
that leads to diabetes.

00:08:53.540 --> 00:09:00.450
And that eventually leads to
people not being able to see,

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:02.810
so that is pretty huge.

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.130
So this model can take
these retinal scans

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:09.420
and identify whether
somebody has that disease

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:13.020
or has indicators of
that disease, and then

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:15.930
at that point, it tells the--

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:19.650
or it alerts this person to
actually go see a doctor.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:22.870
And to me, that is huge.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:26.370
Now I can look into my
phone and just identify

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:28.830
that I might have
a problem, and I

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:32.220
should go see a
doctor is huge when

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:36.300
I don't have a doctor around
me, and somebody is telling me

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.070
I should go do that.

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.210
That, to me, is huge.

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:39.960
LISA QUERA: It is.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.570
I mean if I had
my eyesight saved

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.730
by looking into
a phone camera, I

00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:46.575
think I would feel
it's pretty magical.

00:09:49.134 --> 00:09:50.550
And that's the big
myth of machine

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.462
learning-- that it's magic.

00:09:52.462 --> 00:09:54.420
And I hate to disillusion
you, because we'd all

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.380
like to believe in magic,
but it's just math.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:00.210
It's just linear
algebra and calculus

00:10:00.210 --> 00:10:03.630
combined to transform
inputs into outputs.

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:08.460
And here's another
really key concept

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.200
we want you to walk
away from today--

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.000
that machine learning
is the process

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.839
of algorithmically
finding patterns in data.

00:10:14.839 --> 00:10:17.130
And we're going to keep--
that word "patterns" is going

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:18.270
to keep coming up,
and we're going

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.220
to keep coming back
to the data, because I

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:23.250
want you to think about the
data as the knowledge potential

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:24.600
of what your machine can learn.

00:10:28.137 --> 00:10:30.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: The
concept of machine learning

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.590
has been around for a long
time, and the concept,

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:35.710
in general, for deep
learning, I think,

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:37.984
has existed since like 1960s.

00:10:37.984 --> 00:10:38.650
LISA QUERA: Yes.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.830
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So one
of the questions I had--

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.440
and I'm pretty sure from
the audience as well--

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:48.070
is, why is it that
it's so important now,

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:52.120
and why is it that people are
working towards it now much

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:54.780
more and are getting excited
about this much more now

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:56.830
versus in the past?

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.440
LISA QUERA: Well, there's
three key enablers

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.762
that are making all the
difference now, because you're

00:11:01.762 --> 00:11:02.470
absolutely right.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:04.030
A lot of this isn't new.

00:11:04.030 --> 00:11:05.920
Now we're doing new
things now, and we're

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:08.680
pushing the boundaries of
knowledge all the time.

00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:13.270
But one of the biggest
factors is the data.

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:16.030
90% of the world's data was
generated in the last two

00:11:16.030 --> 00:11:18.364
years, and it's coming
from everywhere.

00:11:18.364 --> 00:11:19.030
It's structured.

00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:19.919
It's unstructured.

00:11:19.919 --> 00:11:20.960
It's coming from the web.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:22.209
It's coming from social media.

00:11:22.209 --> 00:11:25.030
It's coming from ERP
systems and CRM systems,

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:28.300
and it's coming from internet
of things and machine sensors

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:33.310
and phones and just a million of
different options for bringing

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:36.520
the data into your
world and representing

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:39.470
human reality via data points.

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:42.580
Now a lot of that data just
sits there passive now.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:45.130
We're not doing enough
with it, because it

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:47.820
might be in some format that
we don't know how to work with.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:51.910
But you can transform
that and start

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:54.580
pulling insights and
predictive capabilities

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.360
out of that data set.

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:59.470
But if you didn't have those
giant things to learn from,

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:01.780
enough examples
to learn from, we

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.600
wouldn't be able to
build these great models.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:08.020
And the other aspect, of
course, is the computing power.

00:12:08.020 --> 00:12:10.630
You can't work with that much
data and these different models

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:12.010
without the compute power.

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:14.560
In 1961, when the
multilayer perceptron

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:19.930
was originally discovered, the
supercomputer, at the time,

00:12:19.930 --> 00:12:21.852
didn't even hold a candle
to your phone today.

00:12:21.852 --> 00:12:23.810
So it couldn't do anything
with that knowledge.

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.630
We couldn't explore it enough.

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:27.210
So now we can.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:29.510
But of course, what
does it start with?

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:30.220
Data.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:32.454
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Data.

00:12:32.454 --> 00:12:34.870
LISA QUERA: "It's not who has
the best algorithm who wins.

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:36.442
It's who has the most data."

00:12:36.442 --> 00:12:37.900
And that's coming
from a co-founder

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:39.900
of Google Brain and a
former Stanford professor.

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:41.390
Now why do we agree with him?

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:45.400
Why do we think
that that's so true?

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.980
The data determines what
the model will learn.

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:51.490
Remember that it's the
knowledge potential.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.670
If the data does not
accurately, completely,

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:58.030
and fairly represent
the problem environment,

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:02.970
a model cannot find
the optimal solution.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:06.120
Now, when we talk
about completeness,

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.270
what are some of the
things that come into that?

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:09.540
Variety.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:12.690
So you can't just
train it on examples

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:16.800
of a user behaving in one
way or just a single user.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.080
You need a variety of users,
and you need those users

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.190
to behave in a variety of ways.

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:24.940
So diversity of your
dataset is really important.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:27.960
And then, of course, number,
so that's the complete part

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.990
is that we need to make sure
that you have a lot of it.

00:13:35.050 --> 00:13:38.170
Now, the second part is
the quality of the models.

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:40.130
So yes, the ideas have
been around forever,

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.820
but without the ability
to test those ideas

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:46.090
against large datasets and
with a lot of compute power,

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.520
they couldn't identify
the best techniques.

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:50.110
And now we can.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:54.520
So let's start to drill
into how models work,

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:59.611
and we're going to try to
keep this at a good level.

00:13:59.611 --> 00:14:02.110
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Are we going
to go into math and formulas

00:14:02.110 --> 00:14:02.610
and stuff?

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:03.651
LISA QUERA: A little bit.

00:14:03.651 --> 00:14:05.020
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK, awesome.

00:14:05.020 --> 00:14:07.614
The fun part's coming.

00:14:07.614 --> 00:14:10.030
LISA QUERA: But the important
thing, again, conceptually--

00:14:10.030 --> 00:14:12.880
conceptually, we're
applying weights to features

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:15.520
to calculate an output.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:19.810
So if we think about how a
human makes decisions, creates

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:24.709
an output, we also do it by
applying weights to features.

00:14:24.709 --> 00:14:26.500
Let's think about
looking for an apartment,

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:30.100
and how much weight do you
apply to the parking space?

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.920
How much do you apply
to the location?

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.800
These are features of the
apartment that you then

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:37.360
apply different
values to in order

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:41.740
to predict which one
will satisfy your need.

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:43.684
Same idea with machines.

00:14:43.684 --> 00:14:45.850
Now the difference, of
course, is that machines only

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:48.490
work with numeric
data, so we have

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:51.100
to convert any of our
data points into numbers

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:52.870
to work with.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:54.420
So if we have an image--

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:57.270
this one is from the
CIFAR-10 data set,

00:14:57.270 --> 00:15:00.720
so it's a very common data
set used to test out models,

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:05.370
so you can compare models
across different architectures.

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:09.090
So you take the image, and
you convert it to an array

00:15:09.090 --> 00:15:13.960
of the 32 width by 32 height
by the three channels-- red,

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:15.220
green, blue--

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.660
and you end up with
3,072 numbers in total,

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.130
each of those
numbers representing

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:22.800
that pixel feature.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:26.467
So that becomes the x in the
function, the input data.

00:15:26.467 --> 00:15:28.050
And then we have
these weights that we

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:30.630
start to assign to
each of those pixels

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:33.300
to understand its salience.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:38.560
How important is that feature
to calculating the output?

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.190
And in the end, you end up with
the 10 different labels that

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:45.130
are in that data set, and
you're able to identify

00:15:45.130 --> 00:15:46.451
that that's a cat.

00:15:46.451 --> 00:15:48.802
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Awesome.

00:15:48.802 --> 00:15:51.260
LISA QUERA: So let's think
again about this neural network.

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:54.290
We have the concept of
the input and the weight,

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:58.214
and we start to do a
matrix multiplication--

00:15:58.214 --> 00:16:00.380
key term that we're going
to come back to later when

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:01.880
we talk about the
power of hardware,

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:05.900
because matrix multiplications
are compute intensive.

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:09.290
But we have our input, and
then we assign our weights.

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:10.590
That's a product.

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:12.650
And then we add
them together across

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:16.460
the different weighted features
to come up with an output.

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:19.610
We run that output through
an activation function.

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:23.720
Think of the activation function
as being a sorting mechanism.

00:16:23.720 --> 00:16:26.540
So you're sort of sorting
signal from noise.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:28.940
Which of these features
contributes the most

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.430
to determining the output?

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:35.580
And once we have that,
then we do a prediction.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:37.130
So we calculate our first guess.

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:39.330
Now, remember that we
start out knowing nothing,

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.090
so we randomly
initialize the weights,

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:42.530
because we don't know anything.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.700
And then we make our
first prediction,

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.250
which is probably pretty awful.

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:48.860
We compare it with the
label, and that error,

00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:51.202
that difference is
what we use to guide

00:16:51.202 --> 00:16:52.910
how we're going to
update the weight, how

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:54.495
we're going to adjust it.

00:16:54.495 --> 00:16:56.120
And then we keep
doing that iteratively

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.670
across all of these
learning runs,

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:01.187
all of these different
examples, until we've finally

00:17:01.187 --> 00:17:03.020
adjusted that weight
to the point where it's

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:05.900
a very accurate representation
of the knowledge

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:07.730
potential and the
reality that we're

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:10.190
trying to capture
to solve the problem

00:17:10.190 --> 00:17:11.837
or capture the opportunity.

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:17.190
Now here is a supervised
learning problem,

00:17:17.190 --> 00:17:19.920
a simple binary
classification for vision.

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:21.960
But this is the deep part.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:23.460
So now you have
your neural network.

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:26.220
You have your weighted outputs.

00:17:26.220 --> 00:17:30.600
The first layer is just
capturing very simple concepts,

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:32.730
like maybe shading.

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:34.700
You then pass that
to the next layer,

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:37.470
so the outputs of one layer
becomes the inputs to the next.

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:39.780
And that next one
refines the concepts

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.620
and turns those shading
into like lines or shapes.

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:44.370
And then it keeps doing
that along the way

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:47.176
so that the lines and shapes
become eyes and mouths.

00:17:47.176 --> 00:17:49.050
And then the eyes and
the mouths and the ears

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:50.940
become cats versus dogs.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:52.710
And then, finally,
you have that output,

00:17:52.710 --> 00:17:56.794
understanding the classification
and capturing the label.

00:17:56.794 --> 00:17:57.906
Does that make sense?

00:17:57.906 --> 00:17:59.030
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: It does.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:17:59.900
It does.

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:04.700
I think it will be good to go
and visualize this a little bit

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:05.301
for people.

00:18:05.301 --> 00:18:06.050
LISA QUERA: Right.

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:07.675
OK, so let's see an
example so that you

00:18:07.675 --> 00:18:10.820
can start to see that connection
between data and output.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:14.540
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So we've
chosen this example today

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:20.330
mainly to guide us through the
process of how machine learning

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:25.940
actually works and how a machine
actually takes the inputs,

00:18:25.940 --> 00:18:30.380
gets trained, evaluates,
and then predicts.

00:18:30.380 --> 00:18:35.060
And I think AutoML Vision
is a great example,

00:18:35.060 --> 00:18:36.600
because it helps you visualize.

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.600
There are images that are
going into the system,

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:40.430
so it helps you
learn and understand

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.180
what is actually happening.

00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:45.750
So that's the sole purpose
of taking this as an example.

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:47.600
And we're going to
consider ourselves

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:53.150
to be people who are learning
about clouds to understand

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:54.420
the weather patterns.

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:58.280
So let's consider ourselves
as scientists in identifying

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.180
weather patterns.

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:03.890
I have taken some examples here
of images of different clouds,

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:04.850
and they are--

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:07.760
and they're also labeled
with different labels

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:09.900
of the types of clouds.

00:19:09.900 --> 00:19:14.810
Now when you hop into
AutoML Vision interface,

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.610
the first thing you
see is the images,

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:20.510
which is obviously
Lisa has emphasized

00:19:20.510 --> 00:19:23.550
enough about the fact that
data is really important, so.

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:27.020
LISA QUERA: I've never
emphasized that enough ever.

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:29.180
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So we go with this--

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:31.400
as soon as we enter
into the interface,

00:19:31.400 --> 00:19:34.250
we end up putting data in.

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:37.650
And in this case, it's
the images of the clouds.

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:39.830
Now there are two
ways to enter data in.

00:19:39.830 --> 00:19:42.260
One is through
Google Cloud Storage.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:45.050
You could create a
CSV of your links

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:48.560
to all the images
associated with the labels.

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:51.470
And once you have those,
you could upload that CSV

00:19:51.470 --> 00:19:54.920
into the interface, and you'd
have all these images loaded up

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:56.090
in here.

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:58.160
The other way to
upload images is

00:19:58.160 --> 00:20:00.260
to just click on this
Add Image button,

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:04.190
and just use the user
interface to actually upload

00:20:04.190 --> 00:20:04.880
those images.

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:08.460
So those are the two ways to
get your data into the system.

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:11.090
Once you have the
images in, you'll

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:15.510
also be able to filter and see
the number of images in here.

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:16.640
I think this is important.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:20.120
If you see, we have large
number of images for cumulus

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.382
and cirrus and also--

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.420
my god, I can't even read it.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:30.710
So I'm just going to
avoid it, not reading.

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:32.930
So the last three-- we have
a large number of those,

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:36.290
and then you can see
the ones with the altos

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:38.270
are a little bit less.

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:40.200
And we'll come back to this.

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:41.870
That's why I'm
pointing this out.

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:43.380
So you have this dataset.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.160
Now, once you have
the dataset, you

00:20:45.160 --> 00:20:47.900
have the next step,
which is to train

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:49.970
your model using this data.

00:20:49.970 --> 00:20:53.030
When we hit-- we've already,
obviously, trained this model,

00:20:53.030 --> 00:20:54.960
so we're not going
to do that today.

00:20:54.960 --> 00:20:59.840
But when you do it for yourself
with your own image dataset,

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:05.570
you will be able to use the
Train tab to train your model.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:10.430
And then you could use
options to train it

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:14.190
for a longer period of time
or lesser period of time.

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:16.520
And there are
advantages to training

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:20.632
longer, because the
model would learn better.

00:21:20.632 --> 00:21:22.840
Is there anything else you'd
like to add, Lisa, here?

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:24.673
LISA QUERA: No, I think
that's the key point

00:21:24.673 --> 00:21:28.460
in the compute time is that
this is also-- remember, compute

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:28.970
hours.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:31.700
It's not necessarily linear
time, so the two models

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:32.780
that we've trained--

00:21:32.780 --> 00:21:34.670
you can see one has a
one hour compute time.

00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:36.800
But I believe that was
probably about 10 minutes,

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:39.260
and I think that the second
model, even though it

00:21:39.260 --> 00:21:41.780
says two compute hours, because
of distributed processing

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:43.740
and parallel computing,
that, I think,

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.110
was only about 15 minutes.

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:46.470
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK.

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:50.940
Yeah, so make sure
you select the time,

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:55.280
depending on how good
you want the model to be

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.170
and how well-trained
you want it to be.

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:01.220
So after the first two steps
of getting the data in,

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:05.030
training the model, now
we're at the evaluate step,

00:22:05.030 --> 00:22:08.300
which is where you're actually
able to see the metrics.

00:22:08.300 --> 00:22:09.980
And how do you see the metrics?

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:12.470
There are multiple different
metrics that you can see,

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:14.990
but the one that I'd like
to highlight and point out

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:16.886
is the confusion matrix.

00:22:16.886 --> 00:22:18.500
Now, it's not confusing.

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:20.130
It's really just the--

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:22.640
you'd understand why it's
called confusion matrix in just

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:23.430
a little bit.

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:26.480
So when you-- let's
take a step back

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.138
before we dive into
confusion matrix.

00:22:29.138 --> 00:22:33.830
When you add images into Cloud
AutoML Vision, what it does

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:39.440
is it separates out your images
into training dataset and test

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:40.010
dataset.

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:41.960
LISA QUERA: And it does that
automatically, which is great.

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:44.501
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah, so you
don't have to do any of that.

00:22:44.501 --> 00:22:48.680
You just upload, and it'll take
care of the separating for you.

00:22:48.680 --> 00:22:53.240
And in machine
learning for your model

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:56.290
to train on a specific dataset.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:23:00.570
And then it's important
that you have a certain test

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:04.590
dataset ready to train the
model that the model has never

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:05.730
seen before.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:06.840
LISA QUERA: Exactly.

00:23:06.840 --> 00:23:09.594
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And what we
are doing here-- this confusion

00:23:09.594 --> 00:23:17.080
matrix is taking that test
dataset, and then predicting,

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:20.670
and then showing you how
accurate the predictions were.

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:27.030
So in this case, we can see
that it was 90%, 94.7% accurate

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:29.370
for the first two
of them, but then we

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:33.630
see a less accuracy
on the actual stratus

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:35.930
one, which was 46.2%.

00:23:35.930 --> 00:23:40.170
Now, you also see
that 23% of those

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:42.510
were identified as cirrus.

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:45.480
So this could--
and now this allows

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.790
us to actually go
into our dataset

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:51.000
and see what the
problem might be.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:53.790
And it goes back to
Lisa's earlier point.

00:23:53.790 --> 00:23:57.570
It depends on how good your
data is and how much data

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.950
you have supplied to the model.

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:01.680
So if you remember,
when we were looking

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:05.730
at the images and their
labels, the ones with altos

00:24:05.730 --> 00:24:10.630
were very less, so we had very
few examples of those in there.

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:13.480
So that's why our model's
probably getting confused.

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:20.550
So we may need more data
to support those examples.

00:24:20.550 --> 00:24:24.930
And I think that is
the important point

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:28.720
that we wanted to come across,
that data is obviously the key.

00:24:28.720 --> 00:24:31.980
You want to make sure that
whatever you want the machine--

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:34.980
your model to learn,
you've provided

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:40.024
enough examples of that in the
dataset for it to learn from.

00:24:40.024 --> 00:24:40.690
LISA QUERA: Yes.

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:41.731
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Right?

00:24:41.731 --> 00:24:43.770
And then the next step
is obviously prediction,

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:45.700
and we provide you--

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:49.020
you can see here that the
curl command for the REST API.

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:52.740
The AutoML Vision would
generate a REST API for you

00:24:52.740 --> 00:24:56.720
that you could use
to apply into your--

00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:58.770
to make this model
for predictions

00:24:58.770 --> 00:25:02.790
into either your website
or your mobile application,

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:07.260
whatever it is that you
want to use this model into.

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:11.070
Hopefully, that was
a good starting point

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:14.110
in terms of seeing
how all the steps work

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:17.100
from putting the data in
to training to evaluate,

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:20.560
and then starting to
predict with that.

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:22.890
I'll pass it over to
Lisa to give us some more

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:24.990
information on models.

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:27.180
LISA QUERA: Yes, I mean, I
think the important point

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:30.570
that we wanted with that was
this connection between data

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:32.280
and quality.

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:33.660
Now parameters
are what the model

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.420
learns to use to represent
that knowledge potential.

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:41.132
Hyperparameters guide how well
or how poorly the model learns.

00:25:41.132 --> 00:25:42.840
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hyperparameters, Lisa,

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:46.540
has always been something that
I have struggled to understand.

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:49.560
So if we can take some
examples to help our audience

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:50.070
learn this.

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:51.510
LISA QUERA: Yeah,
hyperparameters--

00:25:51.510 --> 00:25:53.343
I've always thought
they should have come up

00:25:53.343 --> 00:25:56.340
with a better term, because
it is a little confusing--

00:25:56.340 --> 00:25:57.340
the what versus the how.

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:58.340
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:00.840
LISA QUERA: But a couple of my
favorites are learning rate,

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:04.560
so the learning rate controls
how much you adjust that weight

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:06.630
during each learning iteration.

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:09.000
So as you're going
through the examples,

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:12.330
how much do you change
that weight up or down

00:26:12.330 --> 00:26:14.370
to reflect the new
information you

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:16.200
got from the latest example?

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:20.160
The latest error signal that
guided how you changed it--

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:23.400
the learning rate says
how much you change it.

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:24.900
So I want you to
imagine that you

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:28.710
have a huge stack of books to
get through to learn something.

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:31.350
Now you could just skim
through them very quickly

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:32.880
and get to the end.

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:35.310
But you'd probably miss
something important,

00:26:35.310 --> 00:26:38.820
and you'd certainly miss
the optimal solution.

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:41.160
Or you could go very
slowly, line by line,

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:42.726
and take notes on each one.

00:26:42.726 --> 00:26:44.850
But then you might only
get through a third or half

00:26:44.850 --> 00:26:47.130
of the books in
the time allowed.

00:26:47.130 --> 00:26:49.840
So you would never
get to a solution.

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:52.350
You need to find the right
learning rate for yourself

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:53.730
as you go through those books.

00:26:53.730 --> 00:26:55.380
The model needs the
right learning rate

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:59.430
to understand how fast or how
slow to adjust those weights.

00:26:59.430 --> 00:27:01.050
If the adjustment
is too big, then it

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:04.120
could jump right over
the optimal weight,

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:06.030
but if it's too small,
it could take too long

00:27:06.030 --> 00:27:08.370
and never converge,
because it'll just

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:10.440
run out of compute time.

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:13.570
So the learning rate
is really important.

00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:15.930
Another one that I like
is the dropout rate,

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:18.580
and I like that, because
it seems counterintuitive,

00:27:18.580 --> 00:27:21.540
but it's actually really in
sync with how humans learn.

00:27:21.540 --> 00:27:23.640
So the dropout rate
is intended to prevent

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:25.650
something called overfitting.

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:27.570
A model that overfits
to the dataset

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:29.250
is a model that has
memorized the data,

00:27:29.250 --> 00:27:31.260
so it can't actually
predict anything new.

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:33.510
It would totally fail on
that test set, that something

00:27:33.510 --> 00:27:35.820
that it's never seen, it fails.

00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:39.690
So we introduce a dropout rate
to basically make the learning

00:27:39.690 --> 00:27:40.380
harder.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:43.440
Periodically, we randomly
zero out some weights.

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:45.460
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Just like how a human--

00:27:45.460 --> 00:27:48.720
just like how I forget after I
read something in like 10 days,

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:50.257
and I need to go
back and read it.

00:27:50.257 --> 00:27:51.090
LISA QUERA: Exactly.

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:53.100
But then you retain
it more, if you

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:55.150
have this interspaced learning.

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:56.910
Well, this works the same way.

00:27:56.910 --> 00:27:59.166
So dropout rate is
another really important

00:27:59.166 --> 00:27:59.790
hyperparameter.

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:01.980
It can impact the
quality of your model.

00:28:01.980 --> 00:28:05.735
So hyperparameters are the how,
and the different combinations

00:28:05.735 --> 00:28:07.110
really make a
difference in terms

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:11.240
of data science productivity.

00:28:11.240 --> 00:28:15.475
Now the AutoML Vision that
we saw is created by a model,

00:28:15.475 --> 00:28:16.850
so it's a model
creating a model,

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:18.790
and that's neural
architecture search net.

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:21.320
And you'll notice that
it's not only more accurate

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:23.380
than the human models--

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:25.910
and these were created by some
of the finest minds in the AI

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:26.630
community.

00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:28.880
You've got the best of Google
and the best of Facebook

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:30.690
creating these kind of things.

00:28:30.690 --> 00:28:34.430
And you'll notice that the
machine one beat them, and not

00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:36.350
only did it beat them
in accuracy, which

00:28:36.350 --> 00:28:38.720
is the quality of the model,
but also the operation,

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:41.360
so the amount of compute time
it took, and the parameters.

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:44.130
It's smaller, so it'll be
able to be served faster.

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:47.087
So it's not only more accurate,
but it's more efficient.

00:28:50.444 --> 00:28:52.610
The last part of the "why
now" that we want to cover

00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:54.830
is the compute power.

00:28:54.830 --> 00:28:57.119
So the ideas aren't new.

00:28:57.119 --> 00:28:59.160
The data that we've been
getting for a few years,

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:03.680
but it's this ability to
process massive amounts of data

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:06.950
with big, complex
models and a variety

00:29:06.950 --> 00:29:09.560
of hyperparameter
combinations that needs

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:12.980
this kind of compute power.

00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:14.984
And these models
can be really large.

00:29:14.984 --> 00:29:16.400
These datasets can
be so large, it

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:21.020
can literally take months to
process those big datasets

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:24.380
through the big models and to
optimize the hyperparameters

00:29:24.380 --> 00:29:25.590
along the way.

00:29:25.590 --> 00:29:29.060
So for a data scientist to
wait months for a result

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:31.850
is a huge productivity impact.

00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:36.500
So if we could
experiment faster,

00:29:36.500 --> 00:29:39.320
then we could create
higher quality models.

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:40.950
And the more compute
power we have,

00:29:40.950 --> 00:29:42.740
the more we can
distribute the training,

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:45.350
and the more we can optimize
each training session

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:48.110
to get that quick iteration
and more experiments

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:48.950
that we can run.

00:29:48.950 --> 00:29:53.690
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So hardware
accelerators basically help

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:55.917
us do the training faster.

00:29:55.917 --> 00:29:58.250
LISA QUERA: Exactly, because
they're optimized for that.

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:01.910
So here is training a large
scale machine translation

00:30:01.910 --> 00:30:04.380
model, and you can just
imagine the size of that model.

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:07.280
This language is so complex,
the knowledge potential

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:10.670
that a machine would have to
capture would be gigantic.

00:30:10.670 --> 00:30:14.570
And in this case, it would be 24
hours being parallel processed

00:30:14.570 --> 00:30:18.740
across 32 GPUs, but because
tensor processing units

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:22.360
optimize that matrix
multiplication we saw earlier,

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:24.500
and it's really specialized
for deep learning

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:27.120
and for the math that is
unique to deep learning,

00:30:27.120 --> 00:30:29.460
you can compress that
to an hour and a half.

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:31.442
So imagine a data
scientist waiting 24 hours

00:30:31.442 --> 00:30:32.900
to get the results
of an experiment

00:30:32.900 --> 00:30:36.572
and make another adjustment
or an hour and a half.

00:30:36.572 --> 00:30:37.655
Big productivity increase.

00:30:40.022 --> 00:30:41.480
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, I think we

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:47.360
have learned the model
and the way models work

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:50.170
and the math behind
it a little bit.

00:30:50.170 --> 00:30:54.080
But I think it'll be good
for us to understand,

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:57.462
what are the things to keep
in mind as people start

00:30:57.462 --> 00:30:59.420
to think about machine
learning and applying it

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:01.285
into their projects?

00:31:01.285 --> 00:31:02.660
LISA QUERA: Well,
the first thing

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.900
is that the model is really--

00:31:04.900 --> 00:31:07.460
it's the most fun piece,
but it's a small piece

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.590
of the overall solution.

00:31:09.590 --> 00:31:11.790
Data, data, data--
so data collection,

00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:13.970
data management,
data understanding

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:16.340
with exploration and analysis
and different feature

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:18.770
engineering to make sure
that your data is accurately

00:31:18.770 --> 00:31:21.200
capturing either the problem
that you want to solve

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:23.379
or the opportunity
you want to capture.

00:31:23.379 --> 00:31:24.920
You need to represent
it in the data,

00:31:24.920 --> 00:31:27.800
and that's going to take
a huge amount of effort.

00:31:27.800 --> 00:31:30.019
The model, then, is a
pretty small part of it,

00:31:30.019 --> 00:31:32.060
but what's important is
once you have that model,

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:33.893
you want to use it in
your business process,

00:31:33.893 --> 00:31:35.640
so that's called
serving the model,

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:37.340
creating the predictions.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:40.760
And you want to make sure
that you're retraining it

00:31:40.760 --> 00:31:43.342
periodically, so you need to
monitor it for its accuracy,

00:31:43.342 --> 00:31:45.800
because it can degrade over
time if the reality that you're

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:48.422
capturing is actually changing.

00:31:48.422 --> 00:31:49.880
So all of these
are pieces that you

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:52.505
have to think about when you're
wanting to use machine learning

00:31:52.505 --> 00:31:54.480
in your business process.

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:57.110
Now some things to consider
are please start simple,

00:31:57.110 --> 00:31:59.600
and then add complexity
only as needed.

00:31:59.600 --> 00:32:03.467
So try some of the preexisting
models like Cloud AI APIs.

00:32:03.467 --> 00:32:05.300
And if that's not getting
you what you need,

00:32:05.300 --> 00:32:08.120
then try an AutoML service
where you can make your--

00:32:08.120 --> 00:32:10.160
more domain specific
by tailoring it

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:11.734
with your own dataset.

00:32:11.734 --> 00:32:14.150
If that's not getting what you
need, you can try something

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:15.608
called transfer
learning, where you

00:32:15.608 --> 00:32:18.020
learn from another model to
jumpstart your own custom

00:32:18.020 --> 00:32:20.140
model.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:22.091
And then, finally,
build a custom model

00:32:22.091 --> 00:32:24.090
with a custom dataset,
but that will, of course,

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:26.950
take the highest skill level
and the most amount of time.

00:32:26.950 --> 00:32:29.520
So you want to do
that only when it

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:31.980
adds real competitive
differentiation for you.

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:35.520
See what you can do with
what's already available first.

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:37.200
Now analyze your
data to ensure you're

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:39.660
accurately and fairly
representing the reality you

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:40.830
want to be learned.

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:43.110
The data is your knowledge
potential, so make sure

00:32:43.110 --> 00:32:45.240
you focus on getting that right.

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:46.740
And then make sure
the data features

00:32:46.740 --> 00:32:48.690
you use to train the
model are available when

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:50.460
you want to use the model.

00:32:50.460 --> 00:32:53.322
So when we talk about accurately
representing the environment,

00:32:53.322 --> 00:32:55.530
let's think about predictive
maintenance for a moment

00:32:55.530 --> 00:32:58.050
when you might have machine
sensors in the field,

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.610
but they're really
impacted by the weather.

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:03.090
So you want to use
weather data to help

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:04.594
make your model smarter.

00:33:04.594 --> 00:33:06.510
You want to increase
that knowledge potential,

00:33:06.510 --> 00:33:09.810
so you use a weather data
set to train your model along

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:11.430
with the sensor data.

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:13.140
But at the time of
prediction, will

00:33:13.140 --> 00:33:15.230
you have that weather
data available?

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:19.079
You need to make sure that what
you train is also what you have

00:33:19.079 --> 00:33:20.120
when you want to predict.

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:25.345
Next steps-- there's
so much more to learn,

00:33:25.345 --> 00:33:27.720
and I really recommend starting
