00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:39.620
And then inside the enterprise,
just think about the problem

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:43.430
you're trying to solve, and what
do you believe most accurately

00:37:43.430 --> 00:37:46.090
and completely
represents that problem?

00:37:46.090 --> 00:37:49.047
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: OK,
that's very helpful.

00:37:49.047 --> 00:37:50.630
So the second question
here is, "Can I

00:37:50.630 --> 00:37:53.000
still build a good
machine learning model

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:56.450
with a small amount of data?"

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:58.222
LISA QUERA: Again, it depends.

00:37:58.222 --> 00:38:00.680
The things that I constantly
repeat are "patterns and data"

00:38:00.680 --> 00:38:01.970
and "it depends."

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:04.880
But you can use
transfer learning,

00:38:04.880 --> 00:38:06.860
because transfer
learning enables

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:09.290
you to use a sort
of jumpstart model

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:12.110
that has already been
trained on a massive dataset.

00:38:12.110 --> 00:38:15.510
And then you can customize it
with a small amount of data.

00:38:15.510 --> 00:38:17.090
So when we think
about the example

00:38:17.090 --> 00:38:19.580
that Priyanka showed
us with AutoML Vision,

00:38:19.580 --> 00:38:21.100
that was a tiny dataset.

00:38:21.100 --> 00:38:23.640
That was 1,800 examples--

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:25.700
really small, which
normally, we'd say,

00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:28.760
no way can a machine learn
from so few examples.

00:38:28.760 --> 00:38:31.130
But because it uses
the NASNet architecture

00:38:31.130 --> 00:38:34.520
in the background and a
trained model from that NASNet,

00:38:34.520 --> 00:38:38.270
it's able to just tailor the
last few layers of the model

00:38:38.270 --> 00:38:40.560
to customize it for
your domain specificity.

00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:42.930
So if you only have a
small amount of data,

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:45.740
then you need to start looking
at transfer learning options.

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:47.281
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
So the key to this

00:38:47.281 --> 00:38:49.370
is going back to
some of the things

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:51.450
to consider points
that you made.

00:38:51.450 --> 00:38:53.720
Start from what's
already available.

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:58.040
Then look at AutoML
maybe, and then start

00:38:58.040 --> 00:38:59.780
to look at transfer learning.

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:03.110
And then if none of those do the
job that you're looking to do,

00:39:03.110 --> 00:39:04.707
then you build your
own custom model.

00:39:04.707 --> 00:39:05.540
LISA QUERA: Exactly.

00:39:05.540 --> 00:39:07.370
And there's things
that you can do

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:10.020
to expand that day-to-day
augmentation options.

00:39:10.020 --> 00:39:12.800
But again, bigger
is better when it

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:14.732
comes to the data
part, which leads us

00:39:14.732 --> 00:39:15.690
to the second question.

00:39:15.690 --> 00:39:17.660
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
The last question--

00:39:17.660 --> 00:39:19.880
"Is bigger always
better for models?"

00:39:19.880 --> 00:39:21.600
LISA QUERA: Well,
there's a trade

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:24.901
off between speed and accuracy,
so it depends on how you define

00:39:24.901 --> 00:39:25.400
"better."

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:27.770
What is it that you
want to optimize for?

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:30.650
If you want to
optimize for accuracy,

00:39:30.650 --> 00:39:33.150
then it can be that
bigger is better.

00:39:33.150 --> 00:39:36.316
But remember that concept we
talked about, overfitting?

00:39:36.316 --> 00:39:37.940
Overfitting means
that it can memorize.

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:40.040
If your model is
too big and complex,

00:39:40.040 --> 00:39:43.160
it might just memorize things
and not be able to generalize.

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:45.590
So sometimes you need
that sort of little bit

00:39:45.590 --> 00:39:47.821
of error in the mix.

00:39:47.821 --> 00:39:49.820
And then the other thing
you need to think about

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:51.519
is not just accuracy but speed.

00:39:51.519 --> 00:39:53.060
The bigger the model,
then the slower

00:39:53.060 --> 00:39:55.197
it will create the inference.

00:39:55.197 --> 00:39:56.780
And if latency is
an issue when you're

00:39:56.780 --> 00:39:58.592
using that in your
business process,

00:39:58.592 --> 00:40:00.050
then a smaller
model might, indeed,

00:40:00.050 --> 00:40:03.350
be much better, even if you
do sacrifice some accuracy.

00:40:03.350 --> 00:40:05.250
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, that's amazing.

00:40:05.250 --> 00:40:07.130
Thank you so much,
everybody, for listening.

00:40:07.130 --> 00:40:08.990
Stay tuned for the next session.

00:40:08.990 --> 00:40:11.780
Bring your teams closer
together with G Suite's meeting

00:40:11.780 --> 00:40:15.050
solutions, and thank you.

